Subject: virtual-net: read a host datagram socket without a backlog

A host datagram socket answered whether a datagram was waiting by
receiving one into a backlog of its own, and a receive then copied out
of it. That is a second place readiness can live, which anything that
looks only at the kernel descriptor - a poll, an epoll set, another
thread - then has to be told about, and it costs an allocation and a
copy per datagram. Leaving the datagram in the kernel is simpler.

Readiness is now one byte peeked, which says a datagram is waiting and
leaves it where it is. A receive reads straight into the caller's
buffer, and a peek copies out of the kernel and leaves the datagram
there, which is what a peek on the socket means. A zero-length datagram
is readable and reports one, as it did, because a count of zero reads
as hangup.

Winsock answers a buffer shorter than the datagram with WSAEMSGSIZE and
reports no sender, where recvfrom(2) hands over the first bytes. For the
probe that error is the answer it was after. A receive on Windows
receives or peeks the whole datagram into a buffer its thread keeps and
copies what fits, so a short receive or peek gets the same bytes and
sender it gets elsewhere. Its readiness is one-shot too, re-armed only
by an operation answering WouldBlock, so a receive or peek that succeeded
is followed by the one-byte probe: another datagram waiting is reported,
and an empty socket re-arms the interest.

